.TH HLSED 1 "2026-09-19" "HLSE 1.0.113" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hlsed \- HLSE resident file-integrity monitor daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hlsed
.B \-\-config
.IR file
.br
.B hlsed
.B \-\-check
.IR file
.br
.B hlsed
.RB [ \-\-version |
.BR \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hlsed
watches one or more directories for new or modified files and runs the
HLSE detection engine on the delta: file-masquerade checks
.RB ( hlse_check_file (3))
and a bounded content scan for leaked credentials
.RB ( hlse_scan_secrets (3)).
It is a poll-based file-integrity monitor: every
.B scan\-interval
seconds it walks each
.B watch
directory and re-scans only files whose
.RI ( path ,
mtime, size) tuple is not already in its in-memory dedup table.
.PP
The design is deliberately conservative: identical code on Linux and
macOS, rootless, no event-API dependency, no threads, no network, and
read-only file access. Untrusted watch-dir entries are opened
O_RDONLY|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK and only ever read, matching the rules of
the one-shot
.BR "hlse_core scan" .
.PP
The daemon runs in the foreground and keeps all state in memory (see
.IR SECURITY.md
for the scoped state carve-out). It never detaches: supervise it with
systemd
.RI ( Type=simple ),
launchd, or
.BR nohup (1)
for background operation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Run the monitor using the directives in
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-\-check " file"
Parse the config and verify every
.B watch
directory exists and is a directory, then exit (0 OK, 2 on error).
Useful in
.B ExecStartPre
for systemd units.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ,\  \-V
Print the HLSE version string.
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Usage.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file shares the format documented in
.BR hlse_core (1)
(\fBkey = value\fR, \fB#\fR comments). These keys drive the daemon;
\fIlog\-file\fR, \fIsyslog\fR, \fIfail\-on\fR, \fIpatterns\fR and the
other shared keys behave exactly as they do for the CLI.
.TP
.BI watch " = " dir
Directory to monitor recursively. Repeat the key for multiple
directories (maximum 8). Symlinked directory entries inside the tree are
never followed.
.TP
.BI scan\-interval " = " seconds
Seconds between sweeps, 1\(en86400. Default 60.
.TP
.BI pid\-file " = " path
When set, the daemon creates the file and holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on it for its lifetime; a second
.B hlsed
with the same pid-file refuses to start (exit 1). Removed on clean
shutdown.
.TP
.BI fail\-on " = " band
Alert threshold: \fBlog|alert|block|isolate\fR or 0\(en100. Daemon
default: 40 (ALERT) \(em lower than the CLI default because a monitor's
job is flagging change, not gating CI.
.TP
.BI log\-file " = " path ,\  syslog " = " bool
Alert sinks; findings are emitted through
.BR hlse_alert_emit (3)
to whichever sinks are enabled, and always echoed on stderr.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM , SIGINT
Finish the in-flight sweep step, remove the pid-file, exit 0.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the config file: new watch list, interval, threshold, alert
sinks, and pid-file take effect without a restart. A parse failure keeps
the previous config and logs the error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean stop after SIGTERM/SIGINT.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure \(em e.g. the pid-file is already locked by another
instance.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or config error (unknown key, missing
.BR watch ,
unreadable watch directory, unwritable log file).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hlsed.conf
Conventional location for the config file.
.SH EXAMPLES
A minimal deployable unit:
.PP
.nf
  # /etc/hlsed.conf
  watch         = /etc
  watch         = /srv/www
  scan-interval = 30
  pid-file      = /run/hlsed.pid
  log-file      = /var/log/hlse/findings.jsonl
  fail-on       = alert
.fi
.PP
.nf
  # systemd unit (foreground model — no forking)
  [Service]
  Type=simple
  ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/hlsed --check /etc/hlsed.conf
  ExecStart=/usr/bin/hlsed --config /etc/hlsed.conf
  Restart=on-failure
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hlse_core (1),
.BR hlse-server (1),
.IR SECURITY.md
(scoped in-memory-state carve-out),
.IR docs/SPECIFICATION.md .
.SH BUGS
The dedup table holds at most 4096 entries per process; once full, the
oldest tuples are evicted and such files may re-alert on a later sweep
(false repeat, never a missed change). The secrets scan reads at most
256 KiB of a file \(em larger files are skipped, not partially scanned.
